course: dictionary
lesson: 10184
title: One word, three jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A tile can be a noun or a verb, so read the words around it to find out which. «The bathroom floor has blue tiles.» You can count tiles, so here tile is a thing: a flat, usually square piece of clay, stone or plastic.
explain: «They tiled the kitchen floor on Saturday.» Now tiled is an action. They covered the floor with tiles. Tile, tiles, tiled and tiling all belong to the same verb, so the ending shows when the action happens.
explain: A tile can also be a small flat piece used in a game, like Scrabble or dominoes. The clues are the game words around it, such as turns, playing or a set of pieces. Check the other words in the sentence before you decide which meaning fits.
fact: A tile is a flat, usually square piece of baked clay, stone or plastic.
fact: Tile can be a noun (a thing) or a verb (an action): tile, tiles, tiled, tiling.
fact: «Blue-tiled» means covered with blue tiles, so the word is describing the Ishtar Gate.
fact: Shapes that fit together with no gaps make a pattern called tessellation, which is what tiling a floor does.
fact: In maths, rows × tiles per row gives the area covered by tiles.
exercise: In «They tiled the kitchen floor on Saturday», what kind of word is tiled? | A noun, meaning a thing | An adjective, describing the floor | *A verb, meaning an action of covering | A number
exercise: A floor is covered with 4 rows of tiles, with 6 tiles in each row. How many tiles are there in total? | 46 | *24 | 10 | 6
exercise: «The moth's scales are laid like roof tiles.» What does this comparison suggest? | Moths live on roofs | *The scales overlap, covering each other like tiles | The scales are made of wood | The scales are very heavy
test: In a game of dominoes, what is a tile? | *A small flat piece used to play | A word on a card | A type of floor covering | A cleaning cloth for the table
test: Which pattern is an example of tiling with no gaps? | A single large circle in the middle of a wall | Stones scattered at random on the ground | *Squares covering a floor edge to edge | Leaves blown across a path
exam: What did the girl in Leeds slip on at the pool? | Her party games | *The wet tiles around the pool edge | A wet moth | A plastic toy
